WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.904
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:05.856 --> 00:00:07.770
MILES WARD: Hi, everybody.

00:00:07.770 --> 00:00:09.300
My name's Miles Ward.

00:00:09.300 --> 00:00:11.460
I run the solutions
architecture practice

00:00:11.460 --> 00:00:13.980
for Google Cloud worldwide.

00:00:13.980 --> 00:00:16.410
And I have to tell you, I have
been working with the team

00:00:16.410 --> 00:00:19.110
at Twitter now for two years,
and I cannot be more proud

00:00:19.110 --> 00:00:23.940
to be on stage to show you and
help you see what it is that

00:00:23.940 --> 00:00:26.460
they are doing
with our platform.

00:00:26.460 --> 00:00:28.260
They have prepared
a presentation

00:00:28.260 --> 00:00:33.420
to really unfold how it is that
they are adopting Google Cloud.

00:00:33.420 --> 00:00:36.816
And I'm just incredibly
excited about this moment.

00:00:36.816 --> 00:00:38.190
So to really take
you through it,

00:00:38.190 --> 00:00:42.540
I want to introduce Derek Lyon,
who runs VP of Engineering

00:00:42.540 --> 00:00:43.650
here at Twitter.

00:00:43.650 --> 00:00:45.155
Thank you so much.

00:00:45.155 --> 00:00:47.780
DEREK LYON: So I'm going to talk
a little bit about our journey

00:00:47.780 --> 00:00:48.690
to the cloud.

00:00:48.690 --> 00:00:50.280
We actually didn't
know initially

00:00:50.280 --> 00:00:51.390
that we were going to do Hadoop.

00:00:51.390 --> 00:00:53.260
That was something we
discovered during the process.

00:00:53.260 --> 00:00:55.176
So I'm going to start
out talking a little bit

00:00:55.176 --> 00:00:56.940
about our infrastructure
in general,

00:00:56.940 --> 00:00:59.950
and how we did the
evaluation here.

00:00:59.950 --> 00:01:02.760
So first of all,
Twitter's infrastructure,

00:01:02.760 --> 00:01:04.860
we were founded in 2006.

00:01:04.860 --> 00:01:05.860
That may seem familiar.

00:01:05.860 --> 00:01:08.580
That's the year that some of the
first cloud services started.

00:01:08.580 --> 00:01:10.163
We were actually
slightly before them.

00:01:10.163 --> 00:01:12.870
So cloud was not a
choice that anybody

00:01:12.870 --> 00:01:15.030
was making at that time.

00:01:15.030 --> 00:01:18.930
Over time we've grown up to be a
global scale application, which

00:01:18.930 --> 00:01:20.134
is quite large.

00:01:20.134 --> 00:01:22.050
There are other larger
applications out there,

00:01:22.050 --> 00:01:25.140
but not very many, actually.

00:01:25.140 --> 00:01:27.300
Twitter, relative to
other large web sites,

00:01:27.300 --> 00:01:29.740
has some very unique performance
and scale considerations.

00:01:29.740 --> 00:01:32.160
Especially, we do a
lot of real-time stuff.

00:01:32.160 --> 00:01:35.950
As you'll see, our data platform
is pretty large as well.

00:01:35.950 --> 00:01:39.090
And over time, over
the last 12 years,

00:01:39.090 --> 00:01:41.070
we've grown our footprint
in our data centers.

00:01:41.070 --> 00:01:42.700
And a lot of it's
built to purpose.

00:01:42.700 --> 00:01:44.250
We have enough
scale that we're not

00:01:44.250 --> 00:01:46.680
buying hardware off the racks.

00:01:46.680 --> 00:01:49.140
We're buying stuff that
is built specifically

00:01:49.140 --> 00:01:50.530
for the workload that's running.

00:01:50.530 --> 00:01:52.740
So it's relatively
efficient, which

00:01:52.740 --> 00:01:54.990
I think makes cloud
adoption a little bit more

00:01:54.990 --> 00:01:57.540
challenging for us
as a decision than it

00:01:57.540 --> 00:01:59.130
would be if you were
a start-up buying

00:01:59.130 --> 00:02:03.270
stuff from a retail website.

00:02:03.270 --> 00:02:05.160
So the strategic
questions that we set out

00:02:05.160 --> 00:02:08.039
to answer-- first of all, what's
the long-term mix of cloud

00:02:08.039 --> 00:02:08.922
in our portfolio.

00:02:08.922 --> 00:02:10.380
We're big enough
that we can afford

00:02:10.380 --> 00:02:13.770
to do stuff relatively cost
effectively in the data center.

00:02:13.770 --> 00:02:15.990
At the same time, doing
zero things on the cloud

00:02:15.990 --> 00:02:17.264
seems probably not enough.

00:02:17.264 --> 00:02:19.680
But there's a pretty wide range
of opinions within Twitter

00:02:19.680 --> 00:02:22.500
around what exactly
that ratio was.

00:02:22.500 --> 00:02:25.710
Second question is which
cloud vendor should we use?

00:02:25.710 --> 00:02:31.190
And obviously the choice is, I
think, well anticipated here.

00:02:31.190 --> 00:02:32.880
But a really
important one for us

00:02:32.880 --> 00:02:34.588
organizationally is
also how do we become

00:02:34.588 --> 00:02:35.760
confident in this decision.

00:02:35.760 --> 00:02:37.380
So there's a range
of opinions inside

00:02:37.380 --> 00:02:40.519
of Twitter around what works
well, what doesn't work well.

00:02:40.519 --> 00:02:42.060
People have had
different experiences

00:02:42.060 --> 00:02:43.500
at different companies.

00:02:43.500 --> 00:02:45.870
How do we actually
collect real data

00:02:45.870 --> 00:02:47.370
and make a real
engineering decision

00:02:47.370 --> 00:02:48.911
about what we're
going to do in a way

00:02:48.911 --> 00:02:51.750
that we can defend and
organize people around.

00:02:51.750 --> 00:02:54.660
And then we started
this evaluation in 2016.

00:02:54.660 --> 00:02:56.589
So Miles mentioned, it's
been about two years.

00:02:56.589 --> 00:02:57.630
We'll share some numbers.

00:02:57.630 --> 00:03:00.696
Some of those are perhaps a
little bit dated right now.

00:03:00.696 --> 00:03:02.070
So as you listen
to the talk, I'd

00:03:02.070 --> 00:03:05.820
encourage you to think about the
process as much as the results.

00:03:05.820 --> 00:03:08.235
And your mileage
will, of course, vary,

00:03:08.235 --> 00:03:11.871
and some of the providers
have improved since then.

00:03:11.871 --> 00:03:14.370
So the more tactical questions
that we're trying to answer--

00:03:14.370 --> 00:03:15.690
and I don't want
to minimize these

00:03:15.690 --> 00:03:16.710
by calling these tactical.

00:03:16.710 --> 00:03:19.084
Some of these are actually
the hardest and most important

00:03:19.084 --> 00:03:20.230
questions.

00:03:20.230 --> 00:03:23.672
Number one is how
feasible is it to move

00:03:23.672 --> 00:03:25.630
either Twitter or some
large portion of Twitter

00:03:25.630 --> 00:03:26.690
to the cloud.

00:03:26.690 --> 00:03:29.620
What's the cost of that?

00:03:29.620 --> 00:03:30.996
How do you identify
the workloads

00:03:30.996 --> 00:03:32.661
that you can actually
separate if you're

00:03:32.661 --> 00:03:35.280
going to do a hybrid cloud model
using pretty mature software

00:03:35.280 --> 00:03:38.610
that hasn't anticipated the
hybrid cloud model previously?

00:03:38.610 --> 00:03:40.890
Do you re-architect
things as you do that?

00:03:40.890 --> 00:03:42.332
Or do you do a lift-and-shift?

00:03:42.332 --> 00:03:44.040
And then how do you
put all this together

00:03:44.040 --> 00:03:47.040
into an actionable plan,
with a budget and a timeline

00:03:47.040 --> 00:03:48.420
and everything else?

00:03:48.420 --> 00:03:51.040
It's a massive problem.

00:03:51.040 --> 00:03:55.710
So 2016, our process maybe is a
little bit different than some

00:03:55.710 --> 00:03:56.210
people's.

00:03:56.210 --> 00:03:59.294
So if you're somebody who
has a renewal on the data

00:03:59.294 --> 00:04:01.710
center coming up, and you need
to make a decision quickly,

00:04:01.710 --> 00:04:05.414
this may not be exactly
the right process for you.

00:04:05.414 --> 00:04:07.080
We had the opportunity
to make a patient

00:04:07.080 --> 00:04:08.970
and a rigorous decision.

00:04:08.970 --> 00:04:11.730
And at our scale small
errors in calculations

00:04:11.730 --> 00:04:14.680
are large numbers in
an absolute sense.

00:04:14.680 --> 00:04:18.029
So you'll see that we try to be
very detailed in our decision

00:04:18.029 --> 00:04:19.660
making.

00:04:19.660 --> 00:04:21.600
So we knew that we
wanted to look at this.

00:04:21.600 --> 00:04:23.820
We started out deciding
that we wanted to analyze

00:04:23.820 --> 00:04:25.164
this on a workload basis.

00:04:25.164 --> 00:04:27.330
So we started out defining
the baseline requirements

00:04:27.330 --> 00:04:29.010
for each of our workloads.

00:04:29.010 --> 00:04:31.680
We actually sent RFPs out to
all the different providers

00:04:31.680 --> 00:04:34.680
that we thought might
be candidates for us.

00:04:34.680 --> 00:04:36.150
And then, in each
case, we analyzed

00:04:36.150 --> 00:04:40.410
how each workload would
run on each provider,

00:04:40.410 --> 00:04:43.980
and then built a
plan, and aggregated.

00:04:43.980 --> 00:04:45.880
Here's a little bit
of the timeline.

00:04:45.880 --> 00:04:47.850
I'll kind of walk through this.

00:04:47.850 --> 00:04:49.927
You can see a lot of the
core engineering work

00:04:49.927 --> 00:04:52.260
that we'll talk about in the
evaluation process was here

00:04:52.260 --> 00:04:54.810
in fall of 2016.

00:04:54.810 --> 00:04:57.090
And then there's quite
a bit of iteration

00:04:57.090 --> 00:05:01.870
and organizational approvals,
and other sorts of things.

00:05:01.870 --> 00:05:07.004
And then where we are now,
we actually just started

00:05:07.004 --> 00:05:07.920
our migration process.

00:05:07.920 --> 00:05:12.020
So April was kind of the
official deal assigned.

00:05:12.020 --> 00:05:13.280
We're doing it.

00:05:13.280 --> 00:05:16.500
And June is effectively when
we had everybody in seats,

00:05:16.500 --> 00:05:19.320
and really kind of
working on this.

00:05:19.320 --> 00:05:21.370
So it's pretty new.

00:05:21.370 --> 00:05:22.470
Yeah.

00:05:22.470 --> 00:05:24.990
So the different workloads
that we looked at,

00:05:24.990 --> 00:05:27.330
this looks probably
pretty comprehensive.

00:05:27.330 --> 00:05:29.460
It was intended to be
pretty comprehensive.

00:05:29.460 --> 00:05:30.970
We looked at real-time systems.

00:05:30.970 --> 00:05:32.640
We looked at NoSQL systems.

00:05:32.640 --> 00:05:35.700
We looked at messaging, object
store database, general purpose

00:05:35.700 --> 00:05:37.710
computing, and batch computing.

00:05:37.710 --> 00:05:40.560
And each one of these, we'll
talk about the evaluation

00:05:40.560 --> 00:05:42.030
process for Hadoop,
because that's

00:05:42.030 --> 00:05:43.260
the one that we
found interesting,

00:05:43.260 --> 00:05:44.885
and that's the one
where we're actually

00:05:44.885 --> 00:05:48.790
going to change what we're doing
as a result of what we found.

00:05:48.790 --> 00:05:51.000
But we did a similar analysis
for each one of these,

00:05:51.000 --> 00:05:53.670
actually.

00:05:53.670 --> 00:05:56.100
So for each of these
workloads, what we looked at

00:05:56.100 --> 00:05:58.470
was kind of two
high-level options.

00:05:58.470 --> 00:06:00.165
Do we just use--

00:06:00.165 --> 00:06:02.290
the provider is doing
something better than we are.

00:06:02.290 --> 00:06:05.410
We should just use what
they're doing, and game over.

00:06:05.410 --> 00:06:07.224
Or should we run our
own service there?

00:06:07.224 --> 00:06:09.390
And if we do run it there,
do we just lift and shift

00:06:09.390 --> 00:06:12.120
what we're doing today, or do
we re-architect in some way,

00:06:12.120 --> 00:06:14.540
because the provider
has some primitives that

00:06:14.540 --> 00:06:17.040
perform differently, or offer
different functionality that's

00:06:17.040 --> 00:06:19.200
interesting to us?

00:06:19.200 --> 00:06:21.607
The interesting note
here at the bottom--

00:06:21.607 --> 00:06:23.940
and this is something I'd
encourage you to think about--

00:06:23.940 --> 00:06:27.060
not every user of that platform
will take the same approach.

00:06:27.060 --> 00:06:29.670
So a very small-scale
database might

00:06:29.670 --> 00:06:32.310
benefit from just using
provider-managed service.

00:06:32.310 --> 00:06:35.080
If it happens to be the
Twitter graph or something,

00:06:35.080 --> 00:06:37.890
it may be a little harder to run
that on the provider service.

00:06:37.890 --> 00:06:39.720
So you may choose to
segment a little bit,

00:06:39.720 --> 00:06:41.730
depending on the scale
complexity and uniqueness

00:06:41.730 --> 00:06:44.040
of the workload.

00:06:44.040 --> 00:06:47.420
So for the evaluation, this
is kind of a generic process.

00:06:47.420 --> 00:06:50.070
This I would, if you're
going to take a photo,

00:06:50.070 --> 00:06:52.292
this is probably a good one.

00:06:52.292 --> 00:06:54.000
So we started out with
functional testing

00:06:54.000 --> 00:06:57.540
to just make sure that all
of our software would run.

00:06:57.540 --> 00:06:59.250
We did find a few
minor bugs there.

00:06:59.250 --> 00:07:01.410
That was a little bit
less interesting, though.

00:07:01.410 --> 00:07:03.660
The core of the
process though was

00:07:03.660 --> 00:07:06.961
doing microbenchmarking for each
resource that we depended on.

00:07:06.961 --> 00:07:09.210
And we used that to then
figure out what the candidate

00:07:09.210 --> 00:07:10.690
configurations would be.

00:07:10.690 --> 00:07:14.640
So which class of storage gives
the performance characteristics

00:07:14.640 --> 00:07:16.890
that we want for this
type of workload.

00:07:16.890 --> 00:07:18.900
Once we had a couple of
candidate configurations

00:07:18.900 --> 00:07:21.510
for each workload, we then
moved on to macrobenchmarking,

00:07:21.510 --> 00:07:23.610
where we actually ran
large-scale application

00:07:23.610 --> 00:07:25.830
benchmarks to actually
see how the workload would

00:07:25.830 --> 00:07:28.524
perform in a realistic way.

00:07:28.524 --> 00:07:29.940
We tried to do
some scale testing.

00:07:29.940 --> 00:07:31.860
In some cases we couldn't
go to full Twitter scale,

00:07:31.860 --> 00:07:33.990
but we tried to go within
an order of magnitude

00:07:33.990 --> 00:07:37.470
if we could, as close
to reality as we could.

00:07:37.470 --> 00:07:39.630
That's actually one that
a lot of people don't do,

00:07:39.630 --> 00:07:41.539
because it costs a
little bit of money.

00:07:41.539 --> 00:07:43.080
But I would encourage
you to do that.

00:07:43.080 --> 00:07:44.200
We did find stuff there.

00:07:44.200 --> 00:07:45.905
We'll talk about that later.

00:07:45.905 --> 00:07:48.030
And then this was kind of
a cool thing that we did.

00:07:48.030 --> 00:07:49.780
Each time we found
something, we showed it

00:07:49.780 --> 00:07:52.320
back to the provider with the
scripts that we were using,

00:07:52.320 --> 00:07:53.220
and everything
else, and asked them

00:07:53.220 --> 00:07:55.011
to validate that the
numbers we were seeing

00:07:55.011 --> 00:07:56.190
are what they expected.

00:07:56.190 --> 00:07:59.520
And then, you know, we continued
to iterate on this process.

00:07:59.520 --> 00:08:03.490
So the kind of core first
run through that process

00:08:03.490 --> 00:08:07.030
was about 2 and 1/2 months to
do this on multiple providers.

00:08:07.030 --> 00:08:09.430
So it actually didn't
take that long.

00:08:09.430 --> 00:08:10.900
I would encourage
you to do that.

00:08:10.900 --> 00:08:13.980
The next-- well
over a year, here--

00:08:13.980 --> 00:08:15.990
was us trying to get to
a very precise answer.

00:08:15.990 --> 00:08:18.200
So if you're looking
for an 80/20 solution,

00:08:18.200 --> 00:08:20.430
the two or three
month approach is

00:08:20.430 --> 00:08:22.290
pretty reasonable for
any 80/20 solution.

00:08:22.290 --> 00:08:25.050
If you need higher
accuracy than that,

00:08:25.050 --> 00:08:28.750
expect to run through this
process a couple of times.

00:08:28.750 --> 00:08:32.929
So coming out of that, we
built an overall cloud plan

00:08:32.929 --> 00:08:35.120
that we wanted to then assess.

00:08:35.120 --> 00:08:36.690
This included
candidate architectures

00:08:36.690 --> 00:08:39.840
for each of these workloads that
was driven by the benchmarking

00:08:39.840 --> 00:08:41.287
that we'd done before.

00:08:41.287 --> 00:08:42.870
From there, we tried
to work backwards

00:08:42.870 --> 00:08:44.700
into what the migration plan
would look like for each

00:08:44.700 --> 00:08:46.320
of those workloads,
the dependencies

00:08:46.320 --> 00:08:48.960
between the workloads on
each other, and the timeline.

00:08:48.960 --> 00:08:51.180
And then we created some
financial projections

00:08:51.180 --> 00:08:54.036
associated with that, which I'll
talk about in just a second.

00:08:54.036 --> 00:08:56.160
And then there's some other
business considerations

00:08:56.160 --> 00:08:57.020
as well.

00:08:57.020 --> 00:08:58.920
So the financial
modeling, this we

00:08:58.920 --> 00:09:01.260
did a pretty elaborate
version of this.

00:09:01.260 --> 00:09:04.020
I'm very grateful to our finance
team for their support in this.

00:09:04.020 --> 00:09:05.819
We looked at a 10-year horizon.

00:09:05.819 --> 00:09:07.860
That seems ridiculously
long, but that's actually

00:09:07.860 --> 00:09:09.660
the asset lifetime of
a lot of the assets

00:09:09.660 --> 00:09:11.430
that you're comparing against--

00:09:11.430 --> 00:09:14.650
like a data center,
that's a typical duration.

00:09:14.650 --> 00:09:17.310
And we did find that if you
look at something shorter term,

00:09:17.310 --> 00:09:19.230
you're very sensitive
to how recently you just

00:09:19.230 --> 00:09:22.017
bought servers, or when
your lease is coming up.

00:09:22.017 --> 00:09:23.850
So it was important to
do a long enough time

00:09:23.850 --> 00:09:27.450
horizon that we weren't subject
to those timing effects.

00:09:27.450 --> 00:09:29.500
We looked at multiple
different cloud scenarios.

00:09:29.500 --> 00:09:31.500
We also looked at our
on-premises scenario.

00:09:31.500 --> 00:09:33.654
And in the on-premises
case, we also

00:09:33.654 --> 00:09:35.070
made sure to layer
in improvements

00:09:35.070 --> 00:09:35.910
that we knew we could do.

00:09:35.910 --> 00:09:38.368
So if there's a tech refresh
that's going to help us there,

00:09:38.368 --> 00:09:41.190
we layered all that in.

00:09:41.190 --> 00:09:43.320
The costs actually
kind of interestingly

00:09:43.320 --> 00:09:45.484
segmented out into
immigration period cost,

00:09:45.484 --> 00:09:47.400
where you're running
duplicate infrastructure,

00:09:47.400 --> 00:09:49.900
and you're very sensitive to
the time of the migration plan,

00:09:49.900 --> 00:09:52.860
and if you can cut things over
at exactly the right time.

00:09:52.860 --> 00:09:55.620
And it's actually quite
constrained and complicated.

00:09:55.620 --> 00:09:58.200
And then the longer term
was kind of past the point

00:09:58.200 --> 00:10:00.720
where we had immediate
visibility into what exactly

00:10:00.720 --> 00:10:02.010
was going to happen.

00:10:02.010 --> 00:10:03.840
So there there's
long term curves,

00:10:03.840 --> 00:10:05.940
like Moore's law for the CPU.

00:10:05.940 --> 00:10:08.220
We looked at full
historical pricing

00:10:08.220 --> 00:10:10.604
for how providers have been
cutting costs and stuff,

00:10:10.604 --> 00:10:13.020
and then tried to layer that
into the long-term projection

00:10:13.020 --> 00:10:14.395
to get a sense
for how costs were

00:10:14.395 --> 00:10:17.089
evolving on a relative basis.

00:10:17.089 --> 00:10:18.630
And then this one
adds a lot of work.

00:10:18.630 --> 00:10:21.090
But it was important for
us, because the numbers

00:10:21.090 --> 00:10:21.780
were so big.

00:10:21.780 --> 00:10:23.640
We actually maintain
two independent models

00:10:23.640 --> 00:10:25.830
with separate
calculation paths, so

00:10:25.830 --> 00:10:29.670
that if somebody makes
an error, we can actually

00:10:29.670 --> 00:10:32.310
see the model error there,
because the two models wouldn't

00:10:32.310 --> 00:10:34.500
converge in that case.

00:10:34.500 --> 00:10:38.190
And getting those to converge
was a fair bit of work.

00:10:38.190 --> 00:10:41.120
So what we found, this was
kind of the key takeaway,

00:10:41.120 --> 00:10:43.610
and then we'll go through
the process that got us here.

00:10:43.610 --> 00:10:47.492
An immediate all-in migration
at Twitter scale was expensive.

00:10:47.492 --> 00:10:49.700
We found it would be more
expensive, especially if we

00:10:49.700 --> 00:10:51.920
didn't optimize as we went.

00:10:51.920 --> 00:10:53.480
It was going to
block a ton of teams

00:10:53.480 --> 00:10:55.820
for a long period of time, and
distract us from other stuff

00:10:55.820 --> 00:10:57.900
that we thought we needed
to work on as a business.

00:10:57.900 --> 00:10:59.420
And there was a lot of
risk associated with that.

00:10:59.420 --> 00:11:00.950
So if we got halfway
through that migration,

00:11:00.950 --> 00:11:02.600
and something was
going sideways,

00:11:02.600 --> 00:11:05.330
that was a pretty big problem.

00:11:05.330 --> 00:11:07.790
What we did discover
coming out of that

00:11:07.790 --> 00:11:10.257
is that in cases where we can
embrace a new architecture

00:11:10.257 --> 00:11:11.840
or transform something
and really take

00:11:11.840 --> 00:11:13.214
advantage of
something that cloud

00:11:13.214 --> 00:11:14.900
is providing that we
don't already have,

00:11:14.900 --> 00:11:17.160
there may be more value there.

00:11:17.160 --> 00:11:19.640
And so we decided to pick
something smaller that we

00:11:19.640 --> 00:11:21.770
actually could do a meaningful
transformation with,

00:11:21.770 --> 00:11:23.070
learn from that.

00:11:23.070 --> 00:11:26.002
And then I'm sure our strategy
will evolve over time.

00:11:26.002 --> 00:11:28.460
Hadoop was the workload that
we picked, because we actually

00:11:28.460 --> 00:11:30.720
found some very
interesting things there.

00:11:30.720 --> 00:11:36.137
And we'll do a re-architecture
as part of the migration.

00:11:36.137 --> 00:11:37.970
And one cool outcome
out of this, actually--

00:11:37.970 --> 00:11:39.950
so there's a range of
opinions about what

00:11:39.950 --> 00:11:42.140
the strategy should be.

00:11:42.140 --> 00:11:44.090
I think it's actually
clarified a lot for us.

00:11:44.090 --> 00:11:45.389
We know what we're moving.

00:11:45.389 --> 00:11:46.805
And we're able to
now fully invest

00:11:46.805 --> 00:11:48.660
in moving that, and doing
a good job with that.

00:11:48.660 --> 00:11:50.540
We also know we're not
moving any time soon.

00:11:50.540 --> 00:11:54.230
And we're able to invest more in
running that in the data center

00:11:54.230 --> 00:11:54.840
as well.

00:11:54.840 --> 00:11:58.444
So it's no longer this
debate about should we

00:11:58.444 --> 00:11:59.360
really invest in that?

00:11:59.360 --> 00:12:01.190
Is that going to
disappear in two years?

00:12:01.190 --> 00:12:02.600
No, it's not going to
disappear in two years.

00:12:02.600 --> 00:12:04.025
It's safe to invest in that.

00:12:04.025 --> 00:12:05.300
And I think as a
result we're actually

00:12:05.300 --> 00:12:06.924
doing a better job
in both environments

00:12:06.924 --> 00:12:07.887
than we had previously.

00:12:07.887 --> 00:12:09.720
So with that, I'm going
to hand off to Dave.

00:12:09.720 --> 00:12:11.636
And he's going to talk
a little bit about what

00:12:11.636 --> 00:12:13.254
we looked at for Hadoop.

00:12:13.254 --> 00:12:14.420
DAVID BECKETT: Hi, everyone.

00:12:14.420 --> 00:12:15.650
I'm Dave.

00:12:15.650 --> 00:12:18.140
I'm a staff site reliability
engineer on the data

00:12:18.140 --> 00:12:18.989
infrastructure side.

00:12:18.989 --> 00:12:20.905
So I'm going to talk to
you about the details.

00:12:20.905 --> 00:12:23.238
And first of all, I'm not
sure if everyone knows Hadoop.

00:12:23.238 --> 00:12:26.300
So I'll try to cover it in
as few slides as possible.

00:12:26.300 --> 00:12:29.470
It's a file system
which is called HDFS.

00:12:29.470 --> 00:12:31.610
It lets you run
distributed applications

00:12:31.610 --> 00:12:33.980
with an API in runtime
that's called YARN.

00:12:33.980 --> 00:12:36.245
And then we have a data
processing framework

00:12:36.245 --> 00:12:37.420
or multiple frameworks.

00:12:37.420 --> 00:12:39.880
MapReduce is one everyone
has probably heard of.

00:12:39.880 --> 00:12:42.020
And there's a lot more to it.

00:12:42.020 --> 00:12:44.060
In general, though,
Hadoop's principles

00:12:44.060 --> 00:12:45.660
are this-- it's a
distributed system.

00:12:45.660 --> 00:12:50.060
So it's built from lots of nodes
or computers operating as one.

00:12:50.060 --> 00:12:51.300
It is horizontally scalable.

00:12:51.300 --> 00:12:54.440
If you want to increase your
capacity in compute or storage,

00:12:54.440 --> 00:12:55.910
you just add more nodes.

00:12:55.910 --> 00:12:56.840
It's a large scale.

00:12:56.840 --> 00:13:00.620
We care about terabytes
and petabytes or larger.

00:13:00.620 --> 00:13:02.710
And we carry up to ten
of thousands of nodes.

00:13:02.710 --> 00:13:05.070
That's a typical
scale for Hadoop.

00:13:05.070 --> 00:13:07.290
Hadoop itself is fault
tolerant and self-healing,

00:13:07.290 --> 00:13:09.990
so it handles failure
of machines and hardware

00:13:09.990 --> 00:13:11.220
and software systems.

00:13:11.220 --> 00:13:12.970
And it runs on
commodity hardware.

00:13:12.970 --> 00:13:16.020
So that's just Hadoop
out of the box.

00:13:16.020 --> 00:13:19.020
But Hadoop is strategically
important for Twitter itself.

00:13:19.020 --> 00:13:22.290
We use it for a large variety
of compute in the company.

00:13:22.290 --> 00:13:23.790
For example, on
the data side, we

00:13:23.790 --> 00:13:27.840
use it to store core data,
the systems, and objects

00:13:27.840 --> 00:13:28.770
such as tweets.

00:13:28.770 --> 00:13:30.810
We all have all our
company logs in there.

00:13:30.810 --> 00:13:33.960
We also use it for backups and
other durable storage cases.

00:13:33.960 --> 00:13:36.510
On the analytics side, where
we're looking into data,

00:13:36.510 --> 00:13:38.569
we do all our
metrics work there,

00:13:38.569 --> 00:13:40.860
and all our aggregates--
accounting, sorting, grouping,

00:13:40.860 --> 00:13:43.140
all that kind of stuff
at large scale, which is

00:13:43.140 --> 00:13:44.620
what we're talking about here.

00:13:44.620 --> 00:13:46.270
And then we have
the insight side,

00:13:46.270 --> 00:13:48.570
which is where we do
things like machine

00:13:48.570 --> 00:13:51.570
learning model training
over vast data sets,

00:13:51.570 --> 00:13:53.910
where we do experiments--
that is, both

00:13:53.910 --> 00:13:56.550
create the experiments
in a computing sense,

00:13:56.550 --> 00:13:58.590
and analyze results
that we have gathered

00:13:58.590 --> 00:14:00.600
from other experiments.

00:14:00.600 --> 00:14:03.420
And we also do this
ad hoc computing,

00:14:03.420 --> 00:14:04.680
or analysis in there.

00:14:04.680 --> 00:14:06.290
So if somebody has
a brilliant idea,

00:14:06.290 --> 00:14:08.040
they write an algorithm,
or piece of code,

00:14:08.040 --> 00:14:09.534
and they want to run it.

00:14:09.534 --> 00:14:10.950
Generally it's a
human doing this.

00:14:10.950 --> 00:14:13.560
Rather than something
super-automated,

00:14:13.560 --> 00:14:17.280
it's usually one-off
or irregularly needed.

00:14:17.280 --> 00:14:20.670
So talking about Hadoop
scale, what do we have?

00:14:20.670 --> 00:14:24.220
We run over a half a million
compute cores in Twitter.

00:14:24.220 --> 00:14:28.650
We have over 300 petabytes of
logical storage in production.

00:14:28.650 --> 00:14:30.990
And our Hadoop
cluster side, we have

00:14:30.990 --> 00:14:33.589
clusters that have gone
up to 12,500 nodes, which

00:14:33.589 --> 00:14:35.130
is some of the larger
Hadoop clusters

00:14:35.130 --> 00:14:37.530
that we know of publicly.

00:14:37.530 --> 00:14:39.679
In terms of the sort of
rate of change of things,

00:14:39.679 --> 00:14:41.720
this is just messages that
are going into Hadoop.

00:14:41.720 --> 00:14:43.920
It doesn't include other things
that the company's doing.

00:14:43.920 --> 00:14:45.840
And we have over a
trillion messages a day

00:14:45.840 --> 00:14:47.720
going into our Hadoop clusters.

00:14:47.720 --> 00:14:51.030
There is actually an Apache
conference talk about that,

00:14:51.030 --> 00:14:54.590
if you want to Google that.

00:14:54.590 --> 00:14:57.290
But the workload that
we have is broken up.

00:14:57.290 --> 00:14:59.690
And here's the kind of the
way that we think about it--

00:14:59.690 --> 00:15:01.430
the cluster types.

00:15:01.430 --> 00:15:04.670
Going from the top down, we
have our real-time clusters.

00:15:04.670 --> 00:15:07.760
These are used for very
high-performance critical event

00:15:07.760 --> 00:15:08.420
processing.

00:15:08.420 --> 00:15:10.250
Its about 10% of our compute.

00:15:10.250 --> 00:15:13.090
We have our regular scheduled
processing production jobs.

00:15:13.090 --> 00:15:15.020
That's core-based compute load.

00:15:15.020 --> 00:15:16.850
That's about 60% of it.

00:15:16.850 --> 00:15:19.400
In the green, we have our
ad-hoc, or one-off queries

00:15:19.400 --> 00:15:20.210
and analysis stuff.

00:15:20.210 --> 00:15:22.170
That's the stuff
that's very spiky--

00:15:22.170 --> 00:15:23.070
about 30%.

00:15:23.070 --> 00:15:24.710
And then we have
our cold clusters,

00:15:24.710 --> 00:15:25.880
which are mostly storage.

00:15:25.880 --> 00:15:28.470
We would have very minimal
compute going on in there.

00:15:28.470 --> 00:15:31.200
And this will come
up in a second.

00:15:31.200 --> 00:15:33.920
So we have challenges based
on all this kind of things

00:15:33.920 --> 00:15:35.570
that we want to do as a company.

00:15:35.570 --> 00:15:37.010
We have the challenge of scale.

00:15:37.010 --> 00:15:39.950
We have significant year-on-year
computing and storage growth

00:15:39.950 --> 00:15:41.820
in the double digits.

00:15:41.820 --> 00:15:43.160
We have hardware to operate.

00:15:43.160 --> 00:15:45.634
We have to design and build
and maintain and operate it.

00:15:45.634 --> 00:15:46.800
We have a capacity planning.

00:15:46.800 --> 00:15:48.560
It's especially hard
for us to predict

00:15:48.560 --> 00:15:51.845
the growth of the compute
in the ad hoc case.

00:15:51.845 --> 00:15:53.990
And we have agility,
and that is responding

00:15:53.990 --> 00:15:56.610
fast to customer requests
when an internal team wants

00:15:56.610 --> 00:16:01.454
us to do something to actually
add storage or compute.

00:16:01.454 --> 00:16:02.870
This is especially
tricky as well,

00:16:02.870 --> 00:16:04.805
also, for the ad hoc case.

00:16:04.805 --> 00:16:06.680
Deployment-- we have to
get this all running.

00:16:06.680 --> 00:16:07.805
We have to keep it running.

00:16:07.805 --> 00:16:10.580
It has to be able to
work at scale in the tens

00:16:10.580 --> 00:16:14.130
of thousands size of clusters.

00:16:14.130 --> 00:16:16.820
And it also has to be able
to be upgraded in flight.

00:16:16.820 --> 00:16:18.759
We don't turn these things off.

00:16:18.759 --> 00:16:21.050
The network, as you can
imagine, we have a lot of data.

00:16:21.050 --> 00:16:22.760
We move it around
between data centers,

00:16:22.760 --> 00:16:25.237
as well as inside the clusters.

00:16:25.237 --> 00:16:27.320
And we also have to think
about disaster recovery.

00:16:27.320 --> 00:16:29.204
We keep a lot of data.

00:16:29.204 --> 00:16:30.620
We want to make
sure it's durable,

00:16:30.620 --> 00:16:33.770
and we have the ability to
survive individual failures.

00:16:38.270 --> 00:16:40.700
So in general, we have
this kind of requirement.

00:16:40.700 --> 00:16:43.760
And I want you to think
about the top two, really.

00:16:43.760 --> 00:16:45.770
We care about network.

00:16:45.770 --> 00:16:47.840
Sustained bandwidth
we can get per core.

00:16:47.840 --> 00:16:52.016
We care about the sort of
bulk part of the network.

00:16:52.016 --> 00:16:53.390
We care about
sustained bandwidth

00:16:53.390 --> 00:16:54.490
per core on disk side.

00:16:54.490 --> 00:16:55.670
Again, it's bulk.

00:16:55.670 --> 00:16:58.220
We care about large
sequential reads and writes.

00:16:58.220 --> 00:16:59.920
Throughput, not latency.

00:16:59.920 --> 00:17:01.310
And we also care about capacity.

00:17:01.310 --> 00:17:04.119
We do have to store
a lot of data.

00:17:04.119 --> 00:17:07.384
So CPU and RAM are not
really our big concerns.

00:17:07.384 --> 00:17:09.050
They're down the list
of things we worry

00:17:09.050 --> 00:17:11.020
about in terms of hardware.

00:17:11.020 --> 00:17:13.460
And we also care about the
consistency of data sets,

00:17:13.460 --> 00:17:15.400
because we are moving
around lots of files,

00:17:15.400 --> 00:17:17.980
and we consider a set of
files to be a dataset.

00:17:17.980 --> 00:17:19.960
And we want it to
arrive or to be

00:17:19.960 --> 00:17:23.150
generated in one consistent
way, so you can actually

00:17:23.150 --> 00:17:25.214
operate on it.

00:17:25.214 --> 00:17:27.339
And here's an example of
the kind of on-premise SKU

00:17:27.339 --> 00:17:31.230
that we have, just a very sketch
of the sort of capacity things

00:17:31.230 --> 00:17:31.980
that we got there.

00:17:31.980 --> 00:17:35.980
So they're a reasonably
modest kind of hardware.

00:17:35.980 --> 00:17:39.250
We all have clusters in the
range of 10 to 20,000 nodes.

00:17:39.250 --> 00:17:42.070
We're moving to 25G network.

00:17:42.070 --> 00:17:44.620
And you can see from our
network numbers at the bottom

00:17:44.620 --> 00:17:46.400
that there's some
particular numbers.

00:17:46.400 --> 00:17:47.530
But notice the peaks.

00:17:47.530 --> 00:17:51.490
We go from a peak, or we go
from average to peak up to 10x.

00:17:51.490 --> 00:17:54.170
And that's not 8, and
that's not unusual.

00:17:54.170 --> 00:17:56.680
So we have lots of loads
that vary quite a lot.

00:17:59.840 --> 00:18:02.600
So going back to the cloud
architectural options

00:18:02.600 --> 00:18:05.270
that we had when we were
looking during the evaluations,

00:18:05.270 --> 00:18:06.800
we could use the
Hadoop as a service

00:18:06.800 --> 00:18:09.080
that the cloud
providers gave you.

00:18:09.080 --> 00:18:13.970
And we could also do our own
Twitter Hadoop on cloud VMs.

00:18:13.970 --> 00:18:16.460
For that, on the
choice number two,

00:18:16.460 --> 00:18:19.450
we have two storage options--

00:18:19.450 --> 00:18:22.190
two storage parts--
the durable storage,

00:18:22.190 --> 00:18:24.920
which we were not going
to ever do anything

00:18:24.920 --> 00:18:27.110
but put it on the
cloud object store;

00:18:27.110 --> 00:18:28.490
and on the scratch storage.

00:18:28.490 --> 00:18:31.130
So Hadoop needs temporary
files, intermediate files

00:18:31.130 --> 00:18:36.860
that are used for calculations
during the MapReduce steps.

00:18:36.860 --> 00:18:37.910
We have choices there.

00:18:37.910 --> 00:18:41.750
With HDFS over the object store,
HDFS over the block store.

00:18:41.750 --> 00:18:45.170
So that's like a file
system approach--

00:18:45.170 --> 00:18:46.290
the next file system.

00:18:46.290 --> 00:18:48.200
And then we can use
HDFS on local disks,

00:18:48.200 --> 00:18:53.140
the local attached
disks of the nodes.

00:18:53.140 --> 00:18:55.020
So this is the plan
that we work through.

00:18:55.020 --> 00:18:57.860
We have our baseline test
that Derek was telling you,

00:18:57.860 --> 00:19:00.690
just sort of to establish what
is the sort of configuration

00:19:00.690 --> 00:19:02.030
that we would want to work on.

00:19:02.030 --> 00:19:05.430
And these are all standard
Hadoop distribution tests

00:19:05.430 --> 00:19:08.520
out of the box, you can get
that measure various low-level

00:19:08.520 --> 00:19:09.690
read and write things.

00:19:09.690 --> 00:19:13.800
And things like Terasort
is a MapReduce application

00:19:13.800 --> 00:19:17.012
that reads a lot of data,
then short shuffles,

00:19:17.012 --> 00:19:18.720
and moves it around
the network, moves it

00:19:18.720 --> 00:19:20.357
from disk into memory and back.

00:19:20.357 --> 00:19:21.440
And then it writes it all.

00:19:21.440 --> 00:19:22.640
But it's very lockstep.

00:19:22.640 --> 00:19:26.800
It's very uniform It
doesn't really generate a--

00:19:26.800 --> 00:19:28.800
it's a repeatable thing,
but it doesn't give you

00:19:28.800 --> 00:19:31.470
something that actually
matches what your production

00:19:31.470 --> 00:19:32.936
workload is.

00:19:32.936 --> 00:19:35.310
So looking to the right, we're
emphasizing the functional

00:19:35.310 --> 00:19:35.810
test.

00:19:35.810 --> 00:19:36.930
This is Gridmix.

00:19:36.930 --> 00:19:40.981
Gridmix again is a
standard part of Hadoop.

00:19:40.981 --> 00:19:43.230
When we used a capture of a
real production workload--

00:19:43.230 --> 00:19:47.730
so we grabbed what happened when
1,000 jobs were running on one

00:19:47.730 --> 00:19:50.130
of our clusters-- one of
our production clusters--

00:19:50.130 --> 00:19:51.750
then we could replay it--

00:19:51.750 --> 00:19:54.180
the reads and writes and
the way that the job worked

00:19:54.180 --> 00:19:55.140
and computed.

00:19:55.140 --> 00:19:57.776
So we actually get a pattern
that's not so lockstep.

00:19:57.776 --> 00:19:59.400
It actually matches
our production load

00:19:59.400 --> 00:20:02.460
with overlapping jobs, and
some slightly slower or longer

00:20:02.460 --> 00:20:04.950
that actually stresses the
system more realistically.

00:20:08.100 --> 00:20:09.914
So the HDFS
configurations we tested,

00:20:09.914 --> 00:20:11.580
we were looking for
these kind of things

00:20:11.580 --> 00:20:16.020
in all of the storage solutions
that we were looking for.

00:20:16.020 --> 00:20:19.830
So we wanted availability in two
regions for our critical data,

00:20:19.830 --> 00:20:21.900
and two zones for other--
for intermediate data

00:20:21.900 --> 00:20:23.460
and other data.

00:20:23.460 --> 00:20:25.260
The data set consistency.

00:20:25.260 --> 00:20:27.096
So this is where we
have a set of files

00:20:27.096 --> 00:20:28.220
that we want to manipulate.

00:20:28.220 --> 00:20:30.330
Let's say 1,000
files form a dataset,

00:20:30.330 --> 00:20:33.180
because even some of our
bigger pieces of data

00:20:33.180 --> 00:20:35.410
are just too big to
manipulate in files.

00:20:35.410 --> 00:20:38.740
I mean, our block size
in Hadoop is 512 meg.

00:20:38.740 --> 00:20:41.370
What we typically have
files that are gigabytes.

00:20:41.370 --> 00:20:43.950
And we have thousands of
those to form a data set.

00:20:43.950 --> 00:20:46.950
So to get a consistent view
of that, cloud providers,

00:20:46.950 --> 00:20:48.589
either you can use
the object store.

00:20:48.589 --> 00:20:50.130
And then you can
use the object store

00:20:50.130 --> 00:20:52.170
with some external
consistency service.

00:20:52.170 --> 00:20:55.020
Those were the two
choices we had.

00:20:55.020 --> 00:20:59.410
So I want to go into the Hadoop
evaluation now in more depth.

00:20:59.410 --> 00:21:02.530
There were two choices
for us on Google Cloud.

00:21:02.530 --> 00:21:04.150
We could use Cloud
Dataproc, which

00:21:04.150 --> 00:21:05.760
is the Hadoop as a service.

00:21:05.760 --> 00:21:08.470
And we could use Compute Engine
with Twitter Hadoop's stack

00:21:08.470 --> 00:21:10.060
on top.

00:21:10.060 --> 00:21:11.839
We decided to start
for the evaluation

00:21:11.839 --> 00:21:14.130
with Dataproc, because it
could be deployed right away.

00:21:14.130 --> 00:21:15.572
It was instantly available.

00:21:15.572 --> 00:21:17.530
And we didn't need to
port any systems in order

00:21:17.530 --> 00:21:18.690
to get it running.

00:21:18.690 --> 00:21:21.000
Its version was similar enough
to our on-premise Hadoop

00:21:21.000 --> 00:21:24.670
that we were OK with it in
terms of matching what we were

00:21:24.670 --> 00:21:26.240
going to be actually using.

00:21:26.240 --> 00:21:28.510
So this is for the evaluation.

00:21:28.510 --> 00:21:30.850
And here is our
actual configuration

00:21:30.850 --> 00:21:33.760
that we use for our
Dataproc testing.

00:21:33.760 --> 00:21:37.570
So we worked with 800 VC
views and two different SKUs

00:21:37.570 --> 00:21:39.940
and one standard 8 and
4, but always the 800

00:21:39.940 --> 00:21:42.910
VCPUs to match what we
were capturing on-premise.

00:21:42.910 --> 00:21:45.360
And we did a scale
test with 8,000 VCPUs

00:21:45.360 --> 00:21:49.360
as well, just to see what
we could do as a peak.

00:21:49.360 --> 00:21:52.240
We modeled average CPU,
and average to peak.

00:21:52.240 --> 00:21:54.856
We turned off or didn't use
any preemptible instances,

00:21:54.856 --> 00:21:56.480
because we wanted
something repeatable.

00:21:56.480 --> 00:21:58.800
We wanted the people to be able
to trust the numbers we had.

00:21:58.800 --> 00:22:00.470
And we chose SKUs,
which you can see

00:22:00.470 --> 00:22:02.350
will roughly match
our on-premise SKUs.

00:22:02.350 --> 00:22:05.590
So we were reasonably
comparing like-to-like.

00:22:09.041 --> 00:22:10.790
So I'm not going to
read out these slides.

00:22:10.790 --> 00:22:12.680
But if you want to know
how to do this yourself,

00:22:12.680 --> 00:22:14.763
you can go through the
slide deck and have a look.

00:22:14.763 --> 00:22:17.555
How to get the Rumen--

00:22:17.555 --> 00:22:19.430
I don't know how you
pronounce that-- the way

00:22:19.430 --> 00:22:21.260
that you can do the
trace capturing stuff.

00:22:21.260 --> 00:22:23.870
There is the command
line we ran to do that.

00:22:23.870 --> 00:22:25.970
You can then use
the Gridmix replay

00:22:25.970 --> 00:22:28.560
command, which is written
there in the Gridmix bundle.

00:22:28.560 --> 00:22:30.080
So I'm not going
to read those out.

00:22:30.080 --> 00:22:32.390
But that's what we used.

00:22:32.390 --> 00:22:34.740
And here's the results.

00:22:34.740 --> 00:22:38.120
So first, n1-standard-8, if you
look at the right-hand column,

00:22:38.120 --> 00:22:39.710
we're comparing the
speed-up that we

00:22:39.710 --> 00:22:43.010
tested in our evaluation
to on-premise.

00:22:43.010 --> 00:22:47.600
So the first line is using SSDs,
which we find was 2x faster,

00:22:47.600 --> 00:22:48.440
but it's expensive.

00:22:48.440 --> 00:22:51.110
You're paying for SSDs.

00:22:51.110 --> 00:22:54.320
The other two ones
are using PD-HDD.

00:22:54.320 --> 00:22:56.480
So, hard drive over PD--

00:22:56.480 --> 00:22:57.920
Persistent Disk.

00:22:57.920 --> 00:23:00.260
And we changed the
Compute Engine types

00:23:00.260 --> 00:23:02.695
to get the balance of
network cores and storage

00:23:02.695 --> 00:23:03.320
that we needed.

00:23:03.320 --> 00:23:05.060
I mentioned earlier
about we were

00:23:05.060 --> 00:23:07.550
caring about our
sustained network per core

00:23:07.550 --> 00:23:09.360
and our disk IO per core.

00:23:09.360 --> 00:23:11.870
So we chose a SKU to match
that and to optimize for that.

00:23:11.870 --> 00:23:16.280
And you can see we just about
matched on-premise performance.

00:23:16.280 --> 00:23:18.890
And so that tells you
we are well-tuned.

00:23:18.890 --> 00:23:20.540
We know what our
on-premise needs are.

00:23:20.540 --> 00:23:23.870
And we built hardware
to optimize to that.

00:23:23.870 --> 00:23:28.200
Similarly, on n1-standard-4,
it was a little bit better.

00:23:28.200 --> 00:23:31.160
But it's pretty much matching
our on-premise speeds,

00:23:31.160 --> 00:23:32.860
even though we changed
the SKU slightly.

00:23:32.860 --> 00:23:33.980
We didn't bother testing there.

00:23:33.980 --> 00:23:35.480
The third line on
the previous slide

00:23:35.480 --> 00:23:39.237
was testing without using
GCS as the durable store.

00:23:39.237 --> 00:23:40.820
But it didn't really
give us anything.

00:23:40.820 --> 00:23:45.230
We do want durability
of our data.

00:23:45.230 --> 00:23:49.880
So benchmark findings,
application benchmarks

00:23:49.880 --> 00:23:50.880
are critical.

00:23:50.880 --> 00:23:55.650
So the MapReduce jobs that
we run have multiple steps--

00:23:55.650 --> 00:23:59.630
map shuffle, putting
things on local disk,

00:23:59.630 --> 00:24:01.820
moving across the
network, writing

00:24:01.820 --> 00:24:05.217
and doing reducer operations,
and then final rights.

00:24:05.217 --> 00:24:06.800
So we recommend that
you should always

00:24:06.800 --> 00:24:09.990
rely on an application
benchmark like Gridmix,

00:24:09.990 --> 00:24:11.540
rather than doing
microbenchmarks,

00:24:11.540 --> 00:24:13.670
because microbenchmarks
will give you the maximums,

00:24:13.670 --> 00:24:15.628
but they will not give
you what you really need

00:24:15.628 --> 00:24:17.360
in terms of sustained compute--

00:24:17.360 --> 00:24:19.650
what is it like working
in a real environment.

00:24:19.650 --> 00:24:23.390
And it's relatively easy
to capture your load

00:24:23.390 --> 00:24:25.190
off some other system.

00:24:25.190 --> 00:24:27.470
And in particular
for Google Cloud,

00:24:27.470 --> 00:24:29.630
we want to emphasize that
you can treat network

00:24:29.630 --> 00:24:31.290
storage like local disk.

00:24:31.290 --> 00:24:32.870
And this is really important.

00:24:32.870 --> 00:24:35.390
Both cloud storage and
PD offered nearly as much

00:24:35.390 --> 00:24:38.840
bandwidth as a direct attached
hard drive on-premise.

00:24:38.840 --> 00:24:41.510
So comparing to
plugging in a drive,

00:24:41.510 --> 00:24:43.380
network storage equivalent.

00:24:43.380 --> 00:24:45.870
And this gives you some
great opportunities.

00:24:45.870 --> 00:24:47.450
So it's a big thing that we got.

00:24:47.450 --> 00:24:51.020
And that was specific
to Google Cloud.

00:24:51.020 --> 00:24:57.030
So we have a few
functional tests from that,

00:24:57.030 --> 00:24:59.380
although we find some of the
things that we worked on.

00:24:59.380 --> 00:25:04.930
Live migration, which is a
great feature of Google Cloud,

00:25:04.930 --> 00:25:07.750
during our Hadoop testing,
we didn't notice this at all.

00:25:07.750 --> 00:25:09.250
During other, more
intense testing

00:25:09.250 --> 00:25:10.300
in different parts
of Twitter, when

00:25:10.300 --> 00:25:11.960
they were doing a
very high request

00:25:11.960 --> 00:25:14.410
per second test for
the caches, they

00:25:14.410 --> 00:25:16.435
did notice it was
milliseconds of things.

00:25:16.435 --> 00:25:17.810
Obviously, we
don't care so much.

00:25:17.810 --> 00:25:20.630
In Hadoop we care about
minutes of compute,

00:25:20.630 --> 00:25:23.230
and lots of throughput.

00:25:23.230 --> 00:25:25.714
We did find some issues with
checksums, which we fixed.

00:25:25.714 --> 00:25:27.130
There's a link to
the public JIRA,

00:25:27.130 --> 00:25:29.690
where we contributed to that
in collaboration with Google.

00:25:29.690 --> 00:25:33.010
And we found a system call
bug on local SSDs which

00:25:33.010 --> 00:25:36.100
Google fixed, I, think within
24 hours of us noticing it,

00:25:36.100 --> 00:25:38.950
so it was a very quick reaction.

00:25:38.950 --> 00:25:40.710
So with that, I want
to hand back over

00:25:40.710 --> 00:25:44.170
to Derek, who's going to go over
the evaluation outcomes for us.

00:25:50.831 --> 00:25:52.830
DEREK LYON: So I think
my headset's working now.

00:25:52.830 --> 00:25:54.120
Great.

00:25:54.120 --> 00:25:55.800
So I'd mentioned
that we'd looked

00:25:55.800 --> 00:25:58.049
at this across a number of
different workloads, Hadoop

00:25:58.049 --> 00:26:00.100
being only one of those.

00:26:00.100 --> 00:26:02.250
And there's a lot of
temptation certainly

00:26:02.250 --> 00:26:04.400
to get the costs to
work, and the migration

00:26:04.400 --> 00:26:05.812
to do a lift-and-shift.

00:26:05.812 --> 00:26:08.270
If you're trying to get out of
a data center on a timeline,

00:26:08.270 --> 00:26:09.870
that may be an
appropriate decision,

00:26:09.870 --> 00:26:12.414
because it leads to
the fastest migration.

00:26:12.414 --> 00:26:14.580
And actually, if you're
worried about duplicate cost

00:26:14.580 --> 00:26:17.250
during the migration
period, that's beneficial.

00:26:17.250 --> 00:26:18.750
In the case of
Twitter, though, when

00:26:18.750 --> 00:26:20.340
we actually ran the
analysis, and especially

00:26:20.340 --> 00:26:22.215
because we looked at
such a long-time horizon

00:26:22.215 --> 00:26:25.680
post-migration, what we
found is that migrating

00:26:25.680 --> 00:26:28.380
to an infrastructure that we
hadn't bothered to optimize for

00:26:28.380 --> 00:26:30.180
was pretty inefficient.

00:26:30.180 --> 00:26:32.430
And it was going to set us
up to then immediately have

00:26:32.430 --> 00:26:35.880
to do a second migration
onto something more optimized

00:26:35.880 --> 00:26:38.250
pretty quickly, with quite
a bit of time pressure,

00:26:38.250 --> 00:26:40.410
because the running
cost of that was

00:26:40.410 --> 00:26:44.230
going to be pretty significant.

00:26:44.230 --> 00:26:46.862
And of course then we had
risks around the overall cost

00:26:46.862 --> 00:26:48.820
and risk and disruption
of that lift-and-shift,

00:26:48.820 --> 00:26:50.580
as I mentioned before.

00:26:50.580 --> 00:26:52.980
In the case of Hadoop,
it had less dependencies

00:26:52.980 --> 00:26:55.980
on the actual production
environment at Twitter.

00:26:55.980 --> 00:26:59.052
So it was easier to separate,
which is actually a big deal.

00:26:59.052 --> 00:27:00.510
You can draw a nice
line around it,

00:27:00.510 --> 00:27:03.685
and move it without having to
move everything all at once.

00:27:03.685 --> 00:27:05.310
And especially when
you think about all

00:27:05.310 --> 00:27:07.630
the different supporting
systems in the data center,

00:27:07.630 --> 00:27:09.963
by the time you actually map
out all those dependencies,

00:27:09.963 --> 00:27:12.810
it's actually really attractive
to not have to bring all those,

00:27:12.810 --> 00:27:14.862
especially at the beginning.

00:27:14.862 --> 00:27:17.070
It was also very helpful
that it was a standard piece

00:27:17.070 --> 00:27:19.564
of open-source software.

00:27:19.564 --> 00:27:21.480
Other people have already
run it on the cloud.

00:27:21.480 --> 00:27:23.670
A lot of the issues
that you might encounter

00:27:23.670 --> 00:27:25.110
have already been addressed.

00:27:25.110 --> 00:27:27.620
In our case, if we like
or don't like something,

00:27:27.620 --> 00:27:29.340
it's easy to change.

00:27:29.340 --> 00:27:31.879
And then for the users
on top of our platform,

00:27:31.879 --> 00:27:33.420
the particulars of
the infrastructure

00:27:33.420 --> 00:27:35.250
are somewhat well
abstracted, because they

00:27:35.250 --> 00:27:39.240
think of themselves as just
running on top of Hadoop.

00:27:39.240 --> 00:27:42.660
And then where this was
particularly interesting

00:27:42.660 --> 00:27:45.990
for us, we see a pretty
big explosion in the ways

00:27:45.990 --> 00:27:48.210
that people want to interact
with data, especially

00:27:48.210 --> 00:27:50.080
on the ad hoc side.

00:27:50.080 --> 00:27:52.650
It used to be that you could
kind of insist that everybody

00:27:52.650 --> 00:27:54.316
writes things a certain
way, and there's

00:27:54.316 --> 00:27:55.597
a very opinionated stack.

00:27:55.597 --> 00:27:57.930
We still believe in that for
the production environment.

00:27:57.930 --> 00:27:59.664
But so many
different people that

00:27:59.664 --> 00:28:01.580
want to access data in
so many different ways,

00:28:01.580 --> 00:28:04.470
especially on the ad hoc side,
that it is very attractive

00:28:04.470 --> 00:28:07.320
to be able to put the data
in a storage system that's

00:28:07.320 --> 00:28:09.060
separated.

00:28:09.060 --> 00:28:10.990
And Google let us do that.

00:28:10.990 --> 00:28:12.450
There's also a
long-term bet here,

00:28:12.450 --> 00:28:15.570
that Google's already pretty
good in big data machine

00:28:15.570 --> 00:28:17.652
learning, other
data-related fields.

00:28:17.652 --> 00:28:19.110
And they're investing
a lot there--

00:28:19.110 --> 00:28:21.300
more than we can afford
to do, certainly.

00:28:21.300 --> 00:28:24.420
And so we're hoping to have
access to that ecosystem.

00:28:24.420 --> 00:28:27.139
Certainly, I
actually came to the,

00:28:27.139 --> 00:28:28.680
back when this event
was called Live,

00:28:28.680 --> 00:28:31.410
and it was 2014 or
something, and it

00:28:31.410 --> 00:28:33.720
was the size of this room.

00:28:33.720 --> 00:28:35.830
It's come a long
way in five years--

00:28:35.830 --> 00:28:36.789
four years, five years.

00:28:36.789 --> 00:28:38.496
And if it continued
to grow the same way,

00:28:38.496 --> 00:28:40.080
we'd be very excited about that.

00:28:43.390 --> 00:28:45.570
So separating compute
and storage for Hadoop,

00:28:45.570 --> 00:28:46.984
why was this interesting to us?

00:28:46.984 --> 00:28:48.900
It allows us to scale
the different dimensions

00:28:48.900 --> 00:28:50.520
independently.

00:28:50.520 --> 00:28:53.850
As Dave mentioned, the relative
balance of storage and compute

00:28:53.850 --> 00:28:56.490
is pretty different, depending
on our different cluster types.

00:28:56.490 --> 00:28:58.770
Especially on the cold side,
it's very storage driven.

00:28:58.770 --> 00:29:01.770
On the ad hoc side, the
compute is very, very spiky

00:29:01.770 --> 00:29:04.199
and irregular and
hard to predict.

00:29:04.199 --> 00:29:06.240
It makes it really easy
for us to separate things

00:29:06.240 --> 00:29:07.650
into multiple
different clusters,

00:29:07.650 --> 00:29:10.050
and have clusters that
are more built to purpose.

00:29:10.050 --> 00:29:12.390
We can mix in Dataproc
in certain situations.

00:29:12.390 --> 00:29:14.454
We can run MapReduce
in other situations.

00:29:14.454 --> 00:29:15.870
We don't have to
try to figure out

00:29:15.870 --> 00:29:18.290
a way to run six different
frameworks on a 10,000 node

00:29:18.290 --> 00:29:21.150
cluster without
breaking anything.

00:29:21.150 --> 00:29:22.350
So that's attractive.

00:29:22.350 --> 00:29:23.880
We also liked the
virtual networking

00:29:23.880 --> 00:29:25.060
and project primitives.

00:29:25.060 --> 00:29:27.780
This is actually very nice from
an organizational perspective,

00:29:27.780 --> 00:29:30.330
where we can have a
coherent set of teams

00:29:30.330 --> 00:29:33.242
has a piece of infrastructure
that's assigned to them.

00:29:33.242 --> 00:29:34.950
They're wrapped in a
network that doesn't

00:29:34.950 --> 00:29:35.991
connect to anything else.

00:29:35.991 --> 00:29:37.620
The bill goes
directly back to them.

00:29:37.620 --> 00:29:39.600
And we're not thinking about
this as a bill for my team

00:29:39.600 --> 00:29:40.099
anymore.

00:29:40.099 --> 00:29:41.880
We're thinking
about this as we're

00:29:41.880 --> 00:29:44.439
helping them run infrastructure
that's serving their needs,

00:29:44.439 --> 00:29:46.230
and they're getting
billed for it directly.

00:29:49.430 --> 00:29:50.930
It's also a lot
easier to manage,

00:29:50.930 --> 00:29:53.780
because a lot of the state
lives in the object store.

00:29:53.780 --> 00:29:56.630
So deployments, there's still
some state in the cluster.

00:29:56.630 --> 00:29:58.526
But deploying is a
lot easier than it

00:29:58.526 --> 00:29:59.900
would be when you
have to upgrade

00:29:59.900 --> 00:30:01.520
thousands and thousands
of nodes in place

00:30:01.520 --> 00:30:02.603
without breaking anything.

00:30:02.603 --> 00:30:04.970
You can spin up another
cluster next to it.

00:30:04.970 --> 00:30:06.330
You can use that for testing.

00:30:06.330 --> 00:30:07.730
It's a huge enabler there.

00:30:07.730 --> 00:30:09.860
But also for rolling
upgrades and stuff,

00:30:09.860 --> 00:30:12.892
it's pretty beneficial.

00:30:12.892 --> 00:30:15.100
And it's nice for us to
treat storage as a commodity.

00:30:15.100 --> 00:30:16.814
We have a lot of
disks, and I'm actually

00:30:16.814 --> 00:30:19.230
very excited about not having
to worry about that quite as

00:30:19.230 --> 00:30:21.190
much anymore, although
we will still continue

00:30:21.190 --> 00:30:22.356
to have quite a few of them.

00:30:27.509 --> 00:30:29.050
I think I hit the
wrong button there.

00:30:29.050 --> 00:30:31.700
There we go.

00:30:31.700 --> 00:30:35.467
So we're looking at moving
the cold and ad hoc clusters.

00:30:35.467 --> 00:30:36.800
I think I mentioned this before.

00:30:36.800 --> 00:30:39.200
The production is staying
in the data center.

00:30:39.200 --> 00:30:41.550
For the cold cluster, it's
pretty straightforward.

00:30:41.550 --> 00:30:43.100
We're going to use
the Cloud storage.

00:30:43.100 --> 00:30:45.170
In our case, actually, we
access the data enough,

00:30:45.170 --> 00:30:46.850
that we're expecting
most of this

00:30:46.850 --> 00:30:48.860
will just be standard storage.

00:30:48.860 --> 00:30:50.900
We're not going to
be using the colder

00:30:50.900 --> 00:30:52.901
storage tiers too
much, we don't think,

00:30:52.901 --> 00:30:54.650
although those may
prove interesting to us

00:30:54.650 --> 00:30:56.360
in the future.

00:30:56.360 --> 00:30:59.060
This is also an environment
where Dataproc works nicely,

00:30:59.060 --> 00:31:01.170
because when we
do run a job here,

00:31:01.170 --> 00:31:03.740
it's usually pretty transitory.

00:31:03.740 --> 00:31:06.650
And scaling is almost
entirely storage driven.

00:31:06.650 --> 00:31:08.870
On the ad hoc side, we're
going to do it a little bit

00:31:08.870 --> 00:31:09.369
differently.

00:31:09.369 --> 00:31:13.766
We're going to actually
run Twitter Hadoop there.

00:31:13.766 --> 00:31:15.640
And here's the stack
that we're going to use.

00:31:15.640 --> 00:31:20.220
So we're running on GCE
using our version of Hadoop.

00:31:20.220 --> 00:31:21.840
There's a couple of
benefits to this.

00:31:21.840 --> 00:31:24.240
One is it minimizes the
amount of difference

00:31:24.240 --> 00:31:25.946
between on-premises
and the cloud,

00:31:25.946 --> 00:31:27.570
so that when we go
to move jobs, that's

00:31:27.570 --> 00:31:29.950
going to be more
straightforward.

00:31:29.950 --> 00:31:32.820
We also believe that we can
get better efficiencies there.

00:31:32.820 --> 00:31:35.130
We run a pretty, high CPU
and memory utilization

00:31:35.130 --> 00:31:37.680
on our clusters, and get
quite a bit of cost advantage

00:31:37.680 --> 00:31:38.510
from that.

00:31:38.510 --> 00:31:41.790
And we're looking to
preserve that where possible.

00:31:41.790 --> 00:31:43.980
We are interested in using
BigQuery and Dataflow

00:31:43.980 --> 00:31:44.910
to some extent.

00:31:44.910 --> 00:31:46.980
For our scale, we've
had trouble figuring out

00:31:46.980 --> 00:31:50.910
a way to get BigQuery to
be cost effective for us.

00:31:50.910 --> 00:31:53.040
So we're using that
tactically for use cases

00:31:53.040 --> 00:31:55.740
where BigQuery is
particularly well suited.

00:31:55.740 --> 00:31:58.620
But we do not have a broad-based
adopt BigQuery for everything

00:31:58.620 --> 00:32:02.390
strategy by any means.

00:32:02.390 --> 00:32:05.204
So the node config that we
ended up using actually for V1

00:32:05.204 --> 00:32:06.870
here is actually
pretty straightforward.

00:32:06.870 --> 00:32:09.020
It looks like an n1-standard-8.

00:32:09.020 --> 00:32:10.940
We're not planning to
use preemptible VMs

00:32:10.940 --> 00:32:12.752
or autoscaling to begin with.

00:32:12.752 --> 00:32:14.210
All of these are
interesting things

00:32:14.210 --> 00:32:16.376
that I expect that we will
explore in time, however.

00:32:20.900 --> 00:32:22.750
So I mentioned that our
production workloads

00:32:22.750 --> 00:32:23.680
are staying on-premises.

00:32:23.680 --> 00:32:25.221
There's a couple of
reasons for that.

00:32:25.221 --> 00:32:27.939
You might ask, if one runs
well, why not the other?

00:32:27.939 --> 00:32:29.980
There's a lot more production
dependencies there.

00:32:29.980 --> 00:32:31.855
We'd have to pull a lot
more stuff with that.

00:32:31.855 --> 00:32:34.300
That's a lot harder
to tease out.

00:32:34.300 --> 00:32:36.010
It also increases our
risk if we do that,

00:32:36.010 --> 00:32:38.380
because we're building a
pretty large-scale environment.

00:32:38.380 --> 00:32:41.340
There's a lot of unique
things that we're going

00:32:41.340 --> 00:32:42.490
to do to get this working.

00:32:42.490 --> 00:32:44.980
We would love not to
have hardcore production

00:32:44.980 --> 00:32:47.660
dependencies on this
infrastructure--

00:32:47.660 --> 00:32:49.630
especially in the near-term.

00:32:49.630 --> 00:32:52.660
The production environment has
a lot more regular CPU usage.

00:32:52.660 --> 00:32:56.020
These are scheduled jobs that
run the same way all the time.

00:32:56.020 --> 00:32:58.990
We can build hardware that's
very well suited to that.

00:32:58.990 --> 00:33:01.540
We can restrict people
to fewer runtimes,

00:33:01.540 --> 00:33:04.060
that are most price performant.

00:33:04.060 --> 00:33:06.319
That's all much more reasonable.

00:33:06.319 --> 00:33:08.110
But we still think that
we can support that

00:33:08.110 --> 00:33:11.360
more efficiently on-premises.

00:33:11.360 --> 00:33:13.727
Strategically, what does
this do for Twitter?

00:33:13.727 --> 00:33:15.560
It gets us to a
next-generation architecture

00:33:15.560 --> 00:33:18.900
for this piece of our stack,
which we're very excited about.

00:33:18.900 --> 00:33:21.800
We're going to be able to
leverage capacity and R&amp;D

00:33:21.800 --> 00:33:23.900
from Google.

00:33:23.900 --> 00:33:26.210
We don't have to carry the
full cost of that ourselves.

00:33:26.210 --> 00:33:28.543
We like a model where we're
investing in the things that

00:33:28.543 --> 00:33:29.950
are key to us.

00:33:29.950 --> 00:33:32.030
But when we have
kind of smaller teams

00:33:32.030 --> 00:33:33.620
that want something
that maybe we

00:33:33.620 --> 00:33:37.550
can't afford to spin up a full
team of a Google-type scale,

00:33:37.550 --> 00:33:40.970
that we have a vendor
solution available there.

00:33:40.970 --> 00:33:43.130
We like participating
in a larger ecosystem.

00:33:43.130 --> 00:33:45.080
It's nice to be here
with all of you.

00:33:45.080 --> 00:33:49.840
We're looking to learn from you,
and get access to more things,

00:33:49.840 --> 00:33:53.414
and kind of what we've done
in our data center so far.

00:33:53.414 --> 00:33:55.580
We like the idea of having
a strategic collaboration

00:33:55.580 --> 00:33:56.300
with Google.

00:33:56.300 --> 00:33:59.114
And there's quite
a few other Twitter

00:33:59.114 --> 00:34:01.280
teams that are going to do
something with the cloud.

00:34:01.280 --> 00:34:03.320
Many of them are much
smaller than we are.

00:34:03.320 --> 00:34:04.850
But by having our
team go first, we

00:34:04.850 --> 00:34:07.622
basically establish
that relationship,

00:34:07.622 --> 00:34:09.330
get everything kind
of going for Twitter,

00:34:09.330 --> 00:34:11.090
make it really easy
to move data there,

00:34:11.090 --> 00:34:12.980
get it easy to spin
up services there--

00:34:12.980 --> 00:34:15.216
all the kind of plumbing
work that needs to happen.

00:34:15.216 --> 00:34:17.090
And then the idea is
that many of those teams

00:34:17.090 --> 00:34:18.620
can now make a little
bit more localized

00:34:18.620 --> 00:34:20.953
decision about whether they
run on the cloud or the data

00:34:20.953 --> 00:34:26.429
center, whereas before that was
hard to do in a localized way.

00:34:26.429 --> 00:34:30.179
So we get a couple of
benefits from this.

00:34:30.179 --> 00:34:32.191
Doing large-scale
ad hoc analysis

00:34:32.191 --> 00:34:33.690
and backfills gets
a lot easier when

00:34:33.690 --> 00:34:34.981
you can leverage that capacity.

00:34:34.981 --> 00:34:37.440
So cold, for example,
doesn't use very much CPU,

00:34:37.440 --> 00:34:41.489
until all of a sudden you
have to rewrite everything.

00:34:41.489 --> 00:34:43.530
And I'm very excited
for the capacity

00:34:43.530 --> 00:34:46.650
problem there to not be mine.

00:34:46.650 --> 00:34:51.610
We avoid HDFS elements, like
on the NameNode for example.

00:34:51.610 --> 00:34:54.960
We have a copy of our data now
off-site, which is attractive.

00:34:54.960 --> 00:34:56.850
Tape backups for us
is not really viable.

00:34:56.850 --> 00:34:58.750
So this actually is.

00:34:58.750 --> 00:35:02.100
It makes it a lot easier for
people to get at the cold data.

00:35:02.100 --> 00:35:04.260
And we're excited to
do more analysis there.

00:35:04.260 --> 00:35:07.710
On the platform side,
we have a minority

00:35:07.710 --> 00:35:10.440
of workloads that benefit from
some sort of compliance regime.

00:35:10.440 --> 00:35:12.875
And that's a little bit
easier to support on Google.

00:35:12.875 --> 00:35:14.250
We like the direct
for chargeback

00:35:14.250 --> 00:35:15.555
that I mentioned for projects.

00:35:15.555 --> 00:35:18.180
Retention management is a little
simpler with some of the tools

00:35:18.180 --> 00:35:20.010
that Google provides.

00:35:20.010 --> 00:35:21.990
And we're excited
to start exploring

00:35:21.990 --> 00:35:25.231
some more of that ecosystem.

00:35:25.231 --> 00:35:27.480
So I'm sure a lot of people
here are doing migrations.

00:35:27.480 --> 00:35:29.229
You're probably wondering
what a migration

00:35:29.229 --> 00:35:30.510
looks like at Twitter.

00:35:30.510 --> 00:35:32.010
In some ways, it's
probably similar,

00:35:32.010 --> 00:35:33.343
in some ways a little different.

00:35:33.343 --> 00:35:34.980
So we have a Google
PSO team that's

00:35:34.980 --> 00:35:38.160
on site with us, a
pretty good-sized team.

00:35:38.160 --> 00:35:40.380
And they're working with
us on a day-to-day basis,

00:35:40.380 --> 00:35:42.639
helping us with the migration.

00:35:42.639 --> 00:35:44.430
It's been actually kind
of a nice marriage.

00:35:44.430 --> 00:35:46.170
We have a lot of engineers
that know a lot about Twitter,

00:35:46.170 --> 00:35:48.240
and the specific software
that we're using.

00:35:48.240 --> 00:35:51.670
The Google PSO team knows a lot
about Google's infrastructure,

00:35:51.670 --> 00:35:54.559
and so far I think it's
been a nice marriage there.

00:35:54.559 --> 00:35:56.850
The network connectivity is
a little different probably

00:35:56.850 --> 00:35:58.830
than when other
people are doing.

00:35:58.830 --> 00:36:02.040
Given our scale, we needed
to do a PNI with Google.

00:36:02.040 --> 00:36:05.600
So we're doing 800 gig
total connectivity.

00:36:05.600 --> 00:36:06.600
This is just for Hadoop.

00:36:06.600 --> 00:36:08.016
This isn't for
Twitter in general.

00:36:08.016 --> 00:36:09.490
This is just for our workloads.

00:36:09.490 --> 00:36:12.330
So that's our
network exclusively.

00:36:12.330 --> 00:36:14.185
And we're copying that
into a whole bunch

00:36:14.185 --> 00:36:15.060
of different buckets.

00:36:15.060 --> 00:36:17.340
In most cases, there's a
roughly one-to-one mapping

00:36:17.340 --> 00:36:19.890
between a data set and a
bucket for us, which makes

00:36:19.890 --> 00:36:21.779
the ACL management very simple.

00:36:21.779 --> 00:36:23.320
So we're going have
a lot of buckets.

00:36:23.320 --> 00:36:26.527
And it's actually nice that
that's very easy to do.

00:36:26.527 --> 00:36:28.610
As I mentioned, we're going
to be splitting ad hoc

00:36:28.610 --> 00:36:29.280
and compute.

00:36:29.280 --> 00:36:31.590
We're also going to be
doing more smaller clusters

00:36:31.590 --> 00:36:32.920
than we do in the data center.

00:36:32.920 --> 00:36:35.125
So I think our
largest ad hoc cluster

00:36:35.125 --> 00:36:37.962
is something like 6,500
nodes at the moment.

00:36:37.962 --> 00:36:40.170
We're going to split that
into three or four clusters

00:36:40.170 --> 00:36:44.160
to start with, and then
possibly more later.

00:36:44.160 --> 00:36:46.410
We are making
internal teams owners

00:36:46.410 --> 00:36:47.970
of these clusters in buckets.

00:36:47.970 --> 00:36:49.620
That's a little
bit easier to do,

00:36:49.620 --> 00:36:51.100
given the cloud infrastructure.

00:36:51.100 --> 00:36:53.630
So when it's a on-premises
infrastructure,

00:36:53.630 --> 00:36:56.580
and you're a platform
team that owns it,

00:36:56.580 --> 00:36:58.290
the idea of giving
somebody root on a box

00:36:58.290 --> 00:37:00.640
that you're administering
is a little terrifying.

00:37:00.640 --> 00:37:02.760
There's a lot more that
we can do there that's

00:37:02.760 --> 00:37:04.980
more flexibly on the cloud.

00:37:04.980 --> 00:37:06.920
IAM has actually taken
a fair bit of work.

00:37:06.920 --> 00:37:08.670
We're not doing anything
that complicated.

00:37:08.670 --> 00:37:10.962
We're a Google Apps
user, and we use LDAP.

00:37:10.962 --> 00:37:12.420
But it's actually
taken quite a bit

00:37:12.420 --> 00:37:14.190
of work to figure
out how to do that.

00:37:14.190 --> 00:37:17.310
Some of that is because
we're doing secure clusters

00:37:17.310 --> 00:37:19.410
as part of this, which
is more complicated

00:37:19.410 --> 00:37:21.600
than a typical migration.

00:37:21.600 --> 00:37:25.000
And then we're constantly
updating the migration capacity

00:37:25.000 --> 00:37:25.500
plans.

00:37:25.500 --> 00:37:27.208
We're still pretty
early in that process,

00:37:27.208 --> 00:37:30.570
so completion is a ways away.

00:37:30.570 --> 00:37:32.776
We have found some limits.

00:37:32.776 --> 00:37:34.400
Quite literally,
there's a limits page.

00:37:34.400 --> 00:37:35.600
And we find that
we're asking to get

00:37:35.600 --> 00:37:36.766
those increased quite a lot.

00:37:39.410 --> 00:37:42.200
We found also that
our internal operating

00:37:42.200 --> 00:37:43.970
model of 10,000
node clusters is not

00:37:43.970 --> 00:37:46.250
something other people seem
to be doing on the cloud.

00:37:46.250 --> 00:37:48.083
I don't know how many
other people are doing

00:37:48.083 --> 00:37:49.919
that in the data center either.

00:37:49.919 --> 00:37:51.710
But we're trying to
adapt somewhat, and run

00:37:51.710 --> 00:37:54.140
smaller clusters there.

00:37:54.140 --> 00:37:57.050
Per scaling,
scaling up works OK.

00:37:57.050 --> 00:37:59.810
Scaling down is a little
bit of an unsolved problem.

00:37:59.810 --> 00:38:01.520
So we're not
confident we're going

00:38:01.520 --> 00:38:04.920
to achieve a ton of elasticity
in the very near term.

00:38:04.920 --> 00:38:07.047
And then we do do some
amount of joint capacity

00:38:07.047 --> 00:38:07.880
planning for Google.

00:38:07.880 --> 00:38:10.850
I'd say order of
10,000 cores is--

00:38:10.850 --> 00:38:13.100
you get some encouragement
to talk to them in advance.

00:38:13.100 --> 00:38:16.000
In an order of 10,000
core surprise asks,

00:38:16.000 --> 00:38:19.860
you get a lot of encouragement
to talk to them in advance.

00:38:19.860 --> 00:38:21.222
So what are we doing now?

00:38:21.222 --> 00:38:22.430
We've got the network set up.

00:38:22.430 --> 00:38:24.440
We're doing a lot
of work with IAM.

00:38:24.440 --> 00:38:26.630
We actually have
dedicated clusters just

00:38:26.630 --> 00:38:27.550
for the replication.

00:38:27.550 --> 00:38:29.810
Those are set up and running.

00:38:29.810 --> 00:38:31.430
We're in the process
of standing up

00:38:31.430 --> 00:38:33.860
the clusters, the
proof-of-concept clusters

00:38:33.860 --> 00:38:36.380
on the cloud that are going
to be the first instance

00:38:36.380 --> 00:38:37.307
of the real cluster.

00:38:37.307 --> 00:38:39.140
And then we have a whole
bunch of supporting

00:38:39.140 --> 00:38:41.820
management software around
it that we're building out.

00:38:41.820 --> 00:38:44.140
We're copying petabytes
of data to the cloud.

00:38:44.140 --> 00:38:47.870
Our ongoing copy is going
to be petabytes a day.

00:38:47.870 --> 00:38:50.690
Plus there's the 300 that
we have to do up front.

00:38:50.690 --> 00:38:52.880
We did have one early use
case that we pulled for.

00:38:52.880 --> 00:38:54.620
This has actually
been a pretty fun one.

00:38:54.620 --> 00:38:57.740
We had an internal team with
a sort of short-term need

00:38:57.740 --> 00:38:59.510
for this use case.

00:38:59.510 --> 00:39:01.010
We learned a lot
from this actually.

00:39:01.010 --> 00:39:02.570
I'd love to give a
full talk on this

00:39:02.570 --> 00:39:04.400
at some point in the future.

00:39:04.400 --> 00:39:08.240
So we surprised Google
with a 100,000 core ask.

00:39:08.240 --> 00:39:09.350
They met it.

00:39:09.350 --> 00:39:11.677
And then we started
hammering GCS with it.

00:39:11.677 --> 00:39:13.010
It's been a learning experience.

00:39:13.010 --> 00:39:15.170
We have about a 15 petabyte
data set that we're

00:39:15.170 --> 00:39:17.270
carrying with Presto there.

00:39:17.270 --> 00:39:20.930
And it's taken a little
work, but it's going.

00:39:20.930 --> 00:39:24.592
And then we've also been
learning a lot about the cloud

00:39:24.592 --> 00:39:27.050
storage connector, and working
with that team very closely.

00:39:30.039 --> 00:39:32.080
So I'll give a couple of
recommendations for you,

00:39:32.080 --> 00:39:34.410
and then some
conclusions for us.

00:39:34.410 --> 00:39:37.660
The recommendations for you
of the tests that we ran,

00:39:37.660 --> 00:39:41.480
I would highly encourage you to
do the application benchmarking

00:39:41.480 --> 00:39:42.880
and the scale testing.

00:39:42.880 --> 00:39:45.280
Some of that seems like it
may have been a bit of a no-op

00:39:45.280 --> 00:39:45.680
on Google, right?

00:39:45.680 --> 00:39:47.770
It's kind of similar
performance to on-prem.

00:39:47.770 --> 00:39:49.330
Do I really need to do this?

00:39:49.330 --> 00:39:52.000
We haven't talked about all the
places that we ran these tests.

00:39:52.000 --> 00:39:55.480
And mileage does vary depending
on where you run this.

00:39:55.480 --> 00:39:58.300
So many of the findings
here are true for Google

00:39:58.300 --> 00:40:00.200
in particular, not for
the cloud in general.

00:40:00.200 --> 00:40:01.080
And these are the
tests that will

00:40:01.080 --> 00:40:02.590
allow you to discover that.

00:40:05.230 --> 00:40:06.580
When you compare pricing--

00:40:06.580 --> 00:40:09.070
this is actually also kind
of a fundamental one--

00:40:09.070 --> 00:40:11.530
there's a temptation to
say I have an 8-core box

00:40:11.530 --> 00:40:14.050
with 64 gigs of RAM.

00:40:14.050 --> 00:40:15.550
I'm going to go to
the pricing page.

00:40:15.550 --> 00:40:17.000
I'm going to see how
much that costs there.

00:40:17.000 --> 00:40:18.520
And that's going to be
my price comparison.

00:40:18.520 --> 00:40:20.530
The performance characteristics
are different enough

00:40:20.530 --> 00:40:22.321
that for any meaningful
workload like this,

00:40:22.321 --> 00:40:24.510
that's not a fair
comparison of costs.

00:40:24.510 --> 00:40:26.146
And we much prefer--

00:40:26.146 --> 00:40:27.520
having done the
application-level

00:40:27.520 --> 00:40:29.394
benchmarking-- to compare
the cost of running

00:40:29.394 --> 00:40:31.510
a workload on both
environments, and then

00:40:31.510 --> 00:40:33.880
allow yourself to architect
it properly, and take

00:40:33.880 --> 00:40:38.830
advantage of better or worse
performance as it occurs.

00:40:38.830 --> 00:40:42.112
For example, when you do
the 8 cores and 64 gigs

00:40:42.112 --> 00:40:44.320
on both sides, you've
completely ignored the network,

00:40:44.320 --> 00:40:47.465
which is kind of secretly one
of the most important pieces.

00:40:47.465 --> 00:40:49.840
It doesn't show up on your
bill, but it affects your bill

00:40:49.840 --> 00:40:51.012
massively.

00:40:51.012 --> 00:40:53.470
So make sure that as you're
testing this and querying this,

00:40:53.470 --> 00:40:57.430
you're looking at
those types of things.

00:40:57.430 --> 00:40:59.841
In terms of
migration planning, I

00:40:59.841 --> 00:41:01.840
think you'll get negotiated
into lift-and-shift.

00:41:01.840 --> 00:41:03.340
And that may be the
right answer if you're

00:41:03.340 --> 00:41:04.548
getting out of a data center.

00:41:04.548 --> 00:41:08.122
But in our case, we
benefited a lot from it

00:41:08.122 --> 00:41:10.330
did take two years to come
up with exactly what we're

00:41:10.330 --> 00:41:11.913
going to do, and
move forward with it.

00:41:11.913 --> 00:41:13.540
But I think we have
a much smarter plan

00:41:13.540 --> 00:41:15.340
than we would have
otherwise, which

00:41:15.340 --> 00:41:18.980
is balancing some short-term
value with speed of execution

00:41:18.980 --> 00:41:21.600
and limited risk.

00:41:21.600 --> 00:41:24.120
So conclusions that
we drew, some of these

00:41:24.120 --> 00:41:27.160
were interesting
findings for us.

00:41:27.160 --> 00:41:29.890
If the network is good
enough, locality matters less.

00:41:29.890 --> 00:41:33.157
And that's very interesting for
certain categories of workload.

00:41:33.157 --> 00:41:34.490
Google's network is pretty good.

00:41:34.490 --> 00:41:37.990
So locality doesn't
matter as much there.

00:41:37.990 --> 00:41:40.510
When the compute side
of things get stateless,

00:41:40.510 --> 00:41:42.400
it's a lot easier to manage.

00:41:42.400 --> 00:41:46.120
And being able to
treat PD like local HDD

00:41:46.120 --> 00:41:48.190
is very empowering
for a lot of things.

00:41:48.190 --> 00:41:52.380
And that one in particular
is not true everywhere.

00:41:52.380 --> 00:41:54.280
Cloud adoption is quite complex.

00:41:54.280 --> 00:41:57.360
So you can imagine the
service graph at Twitter

00:41:57.360 --> 00:41:59.280
is quite complicated,
and it's very difficult

00:41:59.280 --> 00:42:02.605
to disentangle that and figure
out exactly what you can move.

00:42:02.605 --> 00:42:03.480
That's a big problem.

00:42:03.480 --> 00:42:05.010
It takes a lot of thinking.

00:42:05.010 --> 00:42:06.810
The architectural
choices themselves

00:42:06.810 --> 00:42:08.819
are somewhat non-obvious.

00:42:08.819 --> 00:42:10.360
There's various
trade-offs and costs,

00:42:10.360 --> 00:42:11.859
and it takes quite
a bit of analysis

00:42:11.859 --> 00:42:13.650
to figure out what to do there.

00:42:13.650 --> 00:42:15.190
And because of both
of those things,

00:42:15.190 --> 00:42:16.590
it's well worth
planning to spend

00:42:16.590 --> 00:42:19.380
some time doing the analysis.

00:42:19.380 --> 00:42:21.629
And then I think the
concluding thought here is

00:42:21.629 --> 00:42:22.920
we're very new to this process.

00:42:22.920 --> 00:42:25.045
A lot of you are probably
farther along with Google

00:42:25.045 --> 00:42:25.590
than we are.

00:42:25.590 --> 00:42:28.470
We're excited to learn
from you at the conference.

00:42:28.470 --> 00:42:31.350
So if you're doing something
at similar scale, especially,

00:42:31.350 --> 00:42:35.190
and are further along, I would
love to get your business card.

